511 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 78 0 R
/T (Stablecoins now sit at the crossing point of three conversations that used to happen separately: crypto regulation, Treasury market structure and monetary sovereignty. The upside is real: faster, cheaper, more programmable cross-border payments, broader access to dollar liquidity and new settlement rails.)
/E (Stablecoins now sit at the crossing point of three conversations that used to happen separately: crypto regulation, Treasury market structure and monetary sovereignty. The upside is real: faster, cheaper, more programmable cross-border payments, broader access to dollar liquidity and new settlement rails.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
512 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 78 0 R
/T (The risks are just as real, and they are not new ones dressed up in new language. Private money is fragile when redemption depends on confidence. Safe assets can turn unstable when everyone tries to sell them at once. Monetary sovereignty erodes when a foreign unit of account becomes more convenient than the domestic one.)
/E (The risks are just as real, and they are not new ones dressed up in new language. Private money is fragile when redemption depends on confidence. Safe assets can turn unstable when everyone tries to sell them at once. Monetary sovereignty erodes when a foreign unit of account becomes more convenient than the domestic one.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
513 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H5
/P 78 0 R
/T (None of this means stablecoins are about to replace central banks or upend traditional finance. The more precise conclusion is narrower: their credibility rests less on the blockchain underneath them than on reserves, liquidity, redemption, supervision and trust.)
/E (None of this means stablecoins are about to replace central banks or upend traditional finance. The more precise conclusion is narrower: their credibility rests less on the blockchain underneath them than on reserves, liquidity, redemption, supervision and trust.)
/Pg 20 0 R
/K [9]
>>
endobj
