1358 0 obj
<<
/Title (Stablecoins now sit at the crossing point of three conversations that used to happen separately: crypto regulation, Treasury market structure and monetary sovereignty. The upside is real: faster, cheaper, more programmable cross-border payments, broader access to dollar liquidity and new settlement rails.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1214 0 R
/Next 1499 0 R
>>
endobj
1359 0 obj
<<
/Title (None of this means stablecoins are about to replace central banks or upend traditional finance. The more precise conclusion is narrower: their credibility rests less on the blockchain underneath them than on reserves, liquidity, redemption, supervision and trust.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1214 0 R
/Prev 1499 0 R
>>
endobj
1360 0 obj
<<
/Title (The question is no longer whether a token can hold a dollar peg on an exchange. It is who gets to issue private money, what sits behind it, and how these fast-growing private balance sheets interact with public monetary systems. For capital markets, that is the real story.)
/Dest [20 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1048 0 R
/Prev 1214 0 R
/First 1500 0 R
/Last 1501 0 R
/Count -2
/Next 1215 0 R
>>
endobj
