/Title (Stablecoins are exposed to runs because backing and redeemability are not the same thing. An issuer can hold nothing but high-quality T-bills and still be forced to sell them fast if holders rush for the exit. Unlike banks, stablecoin issuers have no deposit insurance and no central bank standing behind them as lender of last resort.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1493 0 R
/First 1599 0 R
/Last 1600 0 R
/Count -9
/Next 1601 0 R
>>
endobj
1577 0 obj
<<
/Title (A major redemption wave at a large USD stablecoin issuer could force T-bill sales into an already stressed market. This is not a prediction of a fiscal crisis. It is a new feedback loop between private digital money and public debt that did not exist a decade ago.)
/Dest [17 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1493 0 R
/Prev 1601 0 R
/First 1602 0 R
/Last 1602 0 R
/Count -1
>>
endobj
1578 0 obj
<<
/Title (The monetary policy angle is subtler. In the narrow sense, stablecoin issuance changes the form money takes, not the quantity of it. The deeper problem shows up elsewhere.)
/Dest [18 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 1494 0 R
/Next 1603 0 R
>>
endobj
1579 0 obj
<<
/Title (That can speed up digital dollarisation, chip away at monetary sovereignty and blunt the effectiveness of domestic policy. If savings and payments move into a foreign unit of account, changes in the local policy rate reach a smaller share of the economy.)
